  "content": {
    "id": "p32-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "Outline the rough steps required to find the equation of the straight line that:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p32-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "has $y$-intercept $b$ and gradient $m$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Use gradient-intercept form: $y=mx+b$.",
          "worked": "The gradient and $y$-intercept are already given, so substitute them directly into gradient-intercept form:\n$$y=mx+b.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "has $x$-intercept $a$ and gradient $m$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p32-q7-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "y=mx-ma=m(x-a)",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p32-q7-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Substitute ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "(a,0)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " into ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "y=mx+c",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " to find ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "c=-ma",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The line passes through $(a,0)$. Substitute this point into $y=mx+c$:\n$$\\begin{align*}0&=ma+c\\\\c&=-ma.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=mx-ma\\\\y&=m(x-a).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q7-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "passes through $P$ and $Q$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p32-q7-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "For distinct points, find ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "m_{PQ}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and use point-gradient form through ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "P",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " or ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "Q",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p32-q7-c-short-vertical",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "If ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "PQ",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " is vertical, use ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=x_P",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Write $P=(x_P,y_P)$ and $Q=(x_Q,y_Q)$, with $P\\ne Q$.\nFor $x_Q\\ne x_P$, find the gradient:\n$$m_{PQ}=\\frac{y_Q-y_P}{x_Q-x_P}.$$\nUse point-gradient form through either point, for example:\n$$y-y_P=m_{PQ}(x-x_P).$$\nIf $x_Q=x_P$, the line is vertical, with equation $x=x_P$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q7-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "passes through $P$ and is parallel to another line that has gradient $m$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Use the same gradient $m$ in point-gradient form through $P$.",
          "worked": "Parallel lines have equal gradients. For $P=(x_P,y_P)$, use:\n$$y-y_P=m(x-x_P).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q7-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "passes through $P$ and has an angle of inclination $\\theta$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p32-q7-e-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Find ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "m=\\tan\\theta",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and use point-gradient form through ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "P",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p32-q7-e-short-vertical",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "For ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\theta=90^\\circ",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", use ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=x_P",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "For a nonvertical line, $m=\\tan\\theta$. With $P=(x_P,y_P)$, use point-gradient form:\n$$y-y_P=(\\tan\\theta)(x-x_P).$$\nIf $\\theta=90^\\circ$, the line is vertical, with equation $x=x_P$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q7-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "passes through $P$ but is perpendicular to another line that has gradient $m$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p32-q7-f-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "For ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "m\\ne0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", use gradient ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "-\\frac{1}{m}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and point-gradient form through ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "P",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p32-q7-f-short-vertical",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "For ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "m=0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", use ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=x_P",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "For $m\\ne0$, perpendicular gradients satisfy:\n$$\\begin{align*}mm'&=-1\\\\m'&=-\\frac{1}{m}.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $P=(x_P,y_P)$, use point-gradient form:\n$$y-y_P=-\\frac{1}{m}(x-x_P).$$\nIf $m=0$, the given line is horizontal, so its perpendicular through $P$ is vertical: $x=x_P$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q7-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "is the perpendicular bisector of $P$ and $Q$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p32-q7-g-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "For distinct points, find midpoint ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "M",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and gradient ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "m_{PQ}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ". Use point-gradient form through ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "M",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " with gradient ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "-\\frac{1}{m_{PQ}}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " when ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "PQ",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " is neither horizontal nor vertical.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p32-q7-g-short-cases",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Horizontal ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "PQ",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " gives ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=x_M",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "; vertical ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "PQ",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " gives ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "y=y_M",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "For distinct points $P=(x_P,y_P)$ and $Q=(x_Q,y_Q)$, find their midpoint:\n$$M=(x_M,y_M)=\\left(\\frac{x_P+x_Q}{2},\\frac{y_P+y_Q}{2}\\right).$$\nIf $PQ$ is neither horizontal nor vertical, find:\n$$m_{PQ}=\\frac{y_Q-y_P}{x_Q-x_P}.$$\nThe perpendicular bisector passes through $M$ with gradient $-\\frac{1}{m_{PQ}}$, so use:\n$$y-y_M=-\\frac{1}{m_{PQ}}(x-x_M).$$\nIf $PQ$ is horizontal, its perpendicular bisector is $x=x_M$. If $PQ$ is vertical, its perpendicular bisector is $y=y_M$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
